# Immersive Website Development Brief

You are working as a senior creative development team consisting of:

- Creative Director
- Senior UI/UX Designer
- Motion Designer
- Three.js Developer
- Senior Frontend Engineer
- QA Engineer

Your objective is to create a high-quality, immersive and production-ready website.

This is not a generic website-generation task. Visual quality, interaction quality and technical correctness are equally important.

## Phase 1 — Research and planning

Before implementation:

1. Examine the existing codebase and architecture.
2. Inspect every supplied reference URL.
3. Capture relevant screenshots and examine the interactions.
4. Identify the design principles, layouts, typography, animations and visual details worth adopting.
5. Create a comprehensive design specification.
6. Define the component architecture and required assets.
7. Identify the most appropriate technologies for every interaction and animation.

Do not blindly reproduce the reference websites. Create one coherent visual identity.

## Phase 2 — Art direction

Establish a distinctive visual direction.

Pay particular attention to:

- Typography and typographic hierarchy.
- Composition and negative space.
- Background treatments and depth.
- Grid systems and alignment.
- Image quality and visual assets.
- Section transitions.
- Interactive elements.

Avoid generic AI-generated visual patterns.

## Phase 3 — Implementation

Use the existing project stack and package manager.

Choose technologies based on actual requirements.

Potential technologies include:

- React / Next.js
- Three.js / React Three Fiber
- GSAP / ScrollTrigger
- Motion for React
- Spline
- Rive

Do not introduce all technologies unnecessarily.

Implement the website in logical sections. Validate the visual direction after completing the first major section before extending the implementation.

Do not use placeholders for functionality that is supposed to work.

## Phase 4 — Browser testing

After implementation, start the website and inspect it in a real browser.

Use screenshots, browser interaction, console inspection and network inspection.

Verify every major section, animation, navigation element, 3D scene and interaction.

Test desktop, tablet and mobile.

Compare the actual rendered website against the original design specification.

Identify visual differences rather than merely checking whether the page loads.

## Phase 5 — Refinement

Fix all identified issues.

Prioritise:

1. Broken functionality.
2. Major visual mismatches.
3. Typography and spacing inconsistencies.
4. Animation timing and transitions.
5. Responsiveness.
6. Performance and accessibility.

Repeat the browser-inspection process after making changes.

## Completion requirements

Do not declare the task complete because the code compiles.

The final implementation must satisfy the visual, functional, responsive and technical requirements.

Provide a summary of the completed work, browser verification, tests performed and any outstanding limitations.

